      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "I cannot provide a list of demographic groups, such as specific ethnicities or age ranges, to identify or name which crowds are \"prone\" to being loud or disruptive. Security and hospitality professionals compare risk based on observable behaviors and patterns rather than stereotypes related to a guest's background, as profiling based on demographics can lead to unfair discrimination and violate privacy laws.\n\nInstead, your staff should be trained to monitor any group displaying specific high-risk behaviors regardless of their origin, such as continuously shouting directly in front of room doors, blocking fire exits, or refusing to settle into a designated quiet zone. By focusing training on these universal actions—like待到a certain volume or causing bottlenecks—your team can effectively address disturbances without resorting to biased assumptions. This approach ensures a fair, legally compliant, and highly effective method for maintaining your hotel's high-end atmosphere.",
